  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nWeak shortwave will bring scattered showers and isolated weak \nt-storms from west to east this morning. This is associated with \nan elevated layer of moisture and modest instability. Showers will\nreach our east by midday. For this afternoon, isolated convection\nmay develop near Sheridan and across southeast MT, especially if \na surface trof stalls in this area. All of this activity should \nremain weak, but have added low pops to cover the potential. \nUpstream, there is a trailing PV lobe tracking from OR into \nsouthern ID. This energy will have a drier air mass to work with \nand be competing with rising heights today, so a low chance of \nweak t-storms further west should be confined to southern \nmountains. Look for high temps in the 90s to near 100F today.\n\nSaturday will be very hot & dry as the ridge amplifies and a surge\nof hotter air arrives from the southwest. 700mb temps rising to \n+16C to +20C will push temps over 100F across the area, smoke \npermitting. Daily records are likely, and August records will be \napproached at western stations (see table at end of discussion). \nHeat-related health impacts are a concern and an Extreme Heat \nWatch remains in effect. In addition to the heat will be afternoon\nhumidities in the 7-12% range. We won't see a lot of wind within \nthe thermal trof, but western areas should see mixed SW winds \ngusting over 20 mph. A Fire Weather Watch remains in effect for \nwestern zones.\n\nA weak cold frontal passage arrives late Saturday night and will\nbring 5-10 degrees of cooling for Sunday. Post-frontal NW-N winds\nwill be somewhat breezy in an area north and east of Forsyth, and\nthis is where fire wx concerns will be greatest on Sunday...though\nadmittedly the westerly winds will be much stronger across\nnorthern MT. RHs Sunday will still bottom out in the teens, after\na very poor recovery Saturday night.\n\nAnother frontal passage arrives Sunday night, bringing the coolest\nair mass we have seen since the beginning of July. As a sharper \nupper trof skirts across northern MT we may see brief showers \nacross the west and north Sunday night (tied to mid level \nfrontogenesis). Monday & Tuesday look dry and notably cooler, with\nhighs in the 70s to lower 80s and seasonably cool nighttime lows.\nFINALLY.\n\nEnsemble consensus is for rising heights and a transition to flat\nridging for the middle of next week. This will yield warming for \nWednesday & Thursday with temps likely to climb back to the lower\n90s. The flat ridge will keep monsoon moisture well to the south,\nso these confidently will be dry days.\n\nSmoke impacts: Post-frontal subsidence and shift to upslope winds\nmay yield higher concentrations of surface-based smoke for the\nlatter half of the weekend into Monday. Reduced air quality is \npossible. Something to monitor over the next few days.\n\nBelow is a summary of Saturday forecast highs, with daily and\nAugust records, at each of our main climate stations.\n\nSITE      8/1 FORECAST   8/1 RECORD      AUG RECORD\nBillings     105         102 (2008)    105 (8/2/2024)\nLivingston   104         101 (2000)    105 (8/5/1961)\nMiles City   104         104 (2008)    110 (8/7/1995)\nSheridan     105         103 (2008)    106 (8/7/1983)\nBaker        102          99 (2022)    108 (8/12/2018)\n\nJKL",
